{
 "id": "2026-10-25-i-to-the-i",
 "topic": "why i^i is a real number",
 "subject": "math",
 "hook_type": "curiosity jump",
 "caption": "Write i as e^(iπ/2) and raise it to the power i: the two i's multiply to −1 and leave e^(−π/2) ≈ 0.2079, a plain real number. Every other value of i^i is real too.\n\nSave this for the next time someone says imaginary numbers aren't real.\n\n#math #complexnumbers #eulersformula #mathematics #calculus #stem #learnmath #science",
 "cover_at": 1.0,
 "hook": "i raised to the power i is a real number.",
 "sub": "Not imaginary. About 0.2079.",
 "beats": [
  {
   "text": "Remember $i$: the number whose square is $-1$.",
   "do": [
    {
     "make": "tex",
     "name": "def",
     "tex": [
      "i",
      "^2",
      "=",
      "-1"
     ],
     "size": 120,
     "at": [
      0,
      3.2
     ],
     "colors": {
      "0": "blue"
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Raising $i$ to an imaginary power sounds like it should give something even stranger.",
   "do": [
    {
     "make": "tex",
     "name": "q",
     "tex": [
      "i",
      "^{i}",
      "=",
      "\\,?"
     ],
     "size": 130,
     "at": [
      0,
      0.6
     ],
     "colors": {
      "0": "blue",
      "1": "yellow",
      "3": "red"
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "do": [
    {
     "clear": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "The key is Euler's formula: $e^{i\\theta}$ is the point at angle $\\theta$ on the unit circle.",
   "do": [
    {
     "make": "tex",
     "name": "eu",
     "tex": [
      "e^{i\\theta}",
      "=",
      "\\cos\\theta",
      "+",
      "i\\sin\\theta"
     ],
     "size": 90,
     "at": [
      0,
      3.6
     ],
     "colors": {
      "0": "yellow"
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Take a quarter turn, $\\theta = \\pi/2$. The cosine is 0 and the sine is 1.",
   "do": [
    {
     "make": "tex",
     "name": "qt",
     "tex": [
      "e^{i\\pi/2}",
      "=",
      "0",
      "+",
      "i\\cdot 1"
     ],
     "size": 90,
     "next_to": "eu",
     "side": "down",
     "buff": 0.7,
     "colors": {
      "0": "yellow"
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "So $i$ itself can be written as $e^{i\\pi/2}$.",
   "do": [
    {
     "make": "tex",
     "name": "key",
     "tex": [
      "i",
      "=",
      "e^{i\\pi/2}"
     ],
     "size": 120,
     "next_to": "qt",
     "side": "down",
     "buff": 0.9,
     "colors": {
      "0": "blue",
      "2": "yellow"
     }
    },
    {
     "make": "box",
     "name": "bk",
     "around": "key",
     "color": "yellow"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "do": [
    {
     "hide": [
      "eu",
      "qt",
      "bk"
     ]
    },
    {
     "move": "key",
     "to": [
      0,
      4.9
     ],
     "scale": 0.7
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Now raise both sides to the power $i$.",
   "do": [
    {
     "make": "tex",
     "name": "s1",
     "tex": [
      "i^{i}",
      "=",
      "\\left(e^{i\\pi/2}\\right)^{i}"
     ],
     "size": 110,
     "at": [
      0,
      2.6
     ],
     "colors": {
      "0": "blue"
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Exponents multiply, and $i \\cdot i = -1$. Watch the $i$'s cancel out of the exponent.",
   "do": [
    {
     "make": "tex",
     "name": "s2",
     "tex": [
      "=",
      "e^{i\\cdot i\\cdot\\pi/2}"
     ],
     "size": 110,
     "next_to": "s1",
     "side": "down",
     "buff": 0.6
    },
    {
     "sfx": "riser"
    },
    {
     "morph": "s2",
     "into": {
      "make": "tex",
      "tex": [
       "=",
       "e^{-\\pi/2}"
      ],
      "size": 110,
      "next_to": "s1",
      "side": "down",
      "buff": 0.6
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "No $i$ left anywhere. It's $e^{-\\pi/2}$, roughly 0.2079: an ordinary real number.",
   "do": [
    {
     "make": "tex",
     "name": "ans",
     "tex": [
      "i^{i}",
      "\\approx",
      "0.2079"
     ],
     "size": 130,
     "at": [
      0,
      -1.6
     ],
     "colors": {
      "0": "blue",
      "2": "green"
     }
    },
    {
     "sfx": "ding"
    },
    {
     "make": "box",
     "name": "ba",
     "around": "ans",
     "color": "green"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "do": [
    {
     "clear": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Strictly, $i$ is also $e^{i(\\pi/2 + 2\\pi k)}$ for any whole number $k$, so $i^i$ has infinitely many values.",
   "do": [
    {
     "make": "tex",
     "name": "all",
     "tex": [
      "i^{i}",
      "=",
      "e^{-\\pi/2 \\,-\\, 2\\pi k}"
     ],
     "size": 110,
     "at": [
      0,
      2.4
     ],
     "colors": {
      "0": "blue"
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "And every single one of them is real.",
   "do": [
    {
     "make": "text",
     "name": "real",
     "text": "all real",
     "size": 72,
     "color": "green",
     "next_to": "all",
     "side": "down",
     "buff": 0.8
    },
    {
     "flash": "all"
    },
    {
     "indicate": "all",
     "color": "green"
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "outro": "Follow for more ideas like this.",
 "post_after": "2026-10-25T11:00:00+05:30",
 "est_duration": 64,
 "visual": "equations",
 "approved": false
}